Z||000004||rogergarrison||02-08-2001||04:33 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MontanaFiero:<BR><B>Could it be a defective "delay module"?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>i shouldnt think so, sounds to me like its making a false connection when he moves the stalk.<P>btw, if i remember right, some certain luxury car used to cycle the wiper every time you started it to clean the windshield. Maybe it was Caddy or Lincoln.<P><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bob Englert||02-08-2001||06:51 PM||||The spurious wiper motion is probably being caused by the delay module or the wiper switch.  In my personal experience the wiper switch has caused most of my problems.  The switch is located INSIDE the steering column and it is what the turn signal stalk is stuck into.<P>Advance Auto or PartsAmerica.com sells a suitable replacement switch for about $55.  It is GP Sorensen P/N DS170.  You will need to remove the steering wheel and lock plate, ignition key switch, stalk, and turn signal switch.  It is a pain to do the first time but not really difficult if you take your time and note how the small parts come out.<P>Good luck... <P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl<p>[This message has been edited by Bob Englert (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||1
